  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Showers and thunderstorms today with the potential for \nheavy rainfall and isolated instances of flash flooding.\n\nLatest surface analysis still depicts a stalled frontal \nboundary off to our south and east. Satellite imagery depicts an\ninflux of moisture into the region east of an approaching upper\ntrough axis. We are starting to see some heavier showers \ndevelop in the right entrance region of a 90 knot upper-level \njet. Low pressure is developing over the Carolina, but remains \nvery loosely developed at this stage. At any rate, all of these \nfeatures are expected to evolve throughout the day, giving a \ncontinuous threat for showers and thunderstorms through the \nafternoon hours. \n\nThe latest SPC mesoanalysis is starting to hone in on an area\ngenerally southeast of the I-95 corridor. PWATs in this region \nremain in the 1.6-1.8 inch range, with a decent overlap of low-\nlevel moisture convergence and PPP. Current forecast calls for \n1-2\" somewhere along/southeast of I-95, but could see some\nlocally higher amounts. As it stands right now, the region of\nconcern should fall just southeast of the metro areas. Sandy\nsoils and antecedent low soil moisture in this region should\nmitigate the flooding threat. However, the one exception will be\nif the heaviest axis either shifts north into St. Marys county,\nwhere recent heavy rains leave that area slightly more\nsusceptible to flooding. Additionally, if the axis does shift\nnorth into the metros, flash flooding could become an issue.\nNo flood watch at this time, as confidence remains higher that\nthe highest rain totals will fall in less susceptible areas. If\nthat changes, one may be considered later this morning. \n\nDuring the late morning and early afternoon hours there could be\na lull, but there remains a chance for pop-up showers and storms\npretty much anywhere during this period. \n\nBy evening, another round of showers and thunderstorms will\napproach the Appalachian Mountains and may make to areas east of\nthe Blue Ridge Mountains overnight into early Sunday. Severe\nthreat is low with these, as they will be running into stable\nsurface air. However, they could bring some heavy rainfall and\nthunder. Some fog may then develop in the valleys and VA\nPiedmont, but it will have to do so prior to the arrival of the\nfront. \n\nBy Sunday morning the cold front will be crossing the area with\ndry air advection ensuing in strengthening NW winds. However,\nthere could still be enough moisture and lift to spark a few\nshowers, but overall drying conditions are expected throughout\nthe day.\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Dry conditions anticipated Sunday through early\nnext week. \n\nDewpoints drop sharply in the wake of the cold front Monday as dry \nair moves into the region from the Midwest. Surface high pressure \nwill begin to build over the Appalachians and track east over the \nnext couple of days, with conditions feeling briefly fall-like as \ntemperatures range slightly below average for August. Overnight\nlows range largely in the 50s throughout the region by Tuesday,\nand sub-60F dewpoints could persist until midweek.\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 3...Warmer and more humid conditions return mid-\nweek, with thunderstorm chances returning by Thursday.\n\nAs the week continues, a broad upper-level trough will begin to form \nover the Great Lakes and track towards the Mid-Atlantic by Thursday. \nAs the associated surface low approaches the region, moist air from \nthe south will flow into the area, bringing back an increase in \nhumidity & temperatures back to late-August normals. As this low \ntracks through, expect shower & thunderstorm chances to increase as \nthe associated cold front tracks offshore by the end of the week. \nMedium-range severe indices still maintain a low-end non-zero\nchance for stronger convection as this front moves through. \nTemperatures rise back into the upper 80s by Thursday.",
